.TH NORNCTL 8 "2026-05-16" "norn-rs 0.3" "norn-rs Manual"
.SH NAME
nornctl \- administer a running nornd daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nornctl
[\fI\-s SOCKET\fR] [\fI\-\-json\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nornctl
is a thin client over the JSON UNIX-socket interface exposed by
.BR nornd (8).
By default it formats output for humans; pass \fB\-\-json\fR for machine-
readable output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIPATH\fR, \fB\-\-socket\fR \fIPATH\fR
Admin socket path. Default: \fI/var/run/norn.sock\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit raw JSON instead of a human-readable table.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show this node's identity (pub_key, address) and connected peer count.
.TP
.B peers
List currently-connected peers with link metrics: lag, jitter, loss rate,
trust score, traffic counters, uptime.
.TP
\fBaddpeer\fR \fIURI\fR
Dial a new peer at the given URI (\fBtcp://host:port\fR). Returns once the
dial has been queued; check \fBpeers\fR to confirm establishment.
.TP
\fBshowaddr\fR \fIPUB_KEY_HEX\fR
Compute the 200::/7 IPv6 address derived from a 64-char hex Ed25519 pub key.
Runs locally; does not touch the admin socket.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Show identity:
.RS
.nf
$ nornctl status
pub_key:    a8b7…
address:    200:1cb8:c754:7af0:e2b6:ba5e:1177:69c0
peer_count: 3
.fi
.RE
.PP
List peers as JSON for consumption by a monitoring system:
.RS
.nf
$ nornctl --json peers | jq '.[].address'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Add a peer at runtime:
.RS
.nf
$ nornctl addpeer tcp://203.0.113.42:9001
dialing: tcp://203.0.113.42:9001
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Could not connect to the socket, or the daemon returned an error response.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nornd (8)
.SH AUTHORS
norn-rs contributors.
